.TH "libnvmf_tid_parse_strict" 9 "libnvmf_tid_parse_strict" "October 2026" "libnvme API manual" LINUX
.SH NAME
libnvmf_tid_parse_strict \- Like libnvmf_tid_parse(), but reject malformed input.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "int" libnvmf_tid_parse_strict
.BI "(struct libnvme_global_ctx *ctx "  ","
.BI "const char *str "  ","
.BI "struct libnvmf_tid **out "  ");"
.SH ARGUMENTS
.IP "ctx" 12
Global context for logging.
.IP "str" 12
Input string.
.IP "out" 12
The allocated TID on success; NULL on error.
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Same as \fBlibnvmf_tid_parse\fP except that a malformed token -- a non-empty bare
token (no '='), an empty value, or an unrecognized key -- fails the whole
parse instead of being skipped.  Empty tokens (from ";;" or a trailing ';')
are still benign.  Useful when an unrecognized key should be treated as an
error (e.g. a typo in a hand-edited config) rather than silently ignored.
.SH "RETURN"
0 on success (*\fIout\fP is the allocated TID); -EINVAL if \fIctx\fP, \fIout\fP or
\fIstr\fP is NULL, or on a malformed token; -ENOMEM on allocation failure.
